 Uses 
{{Main|Apricot}}
Seeds or kernels of the apricot grown in central Asia and around the Mediterranean may be substituted for bitter almonds.{{Citation needed|date=October 2013}} The Italian liqueur amaretto and amaretti biscotti are flavoured with extract of apricot kernels rather than almonds.{{Citation needed|date=October 2013}} Oil pressed from these cultivar kernels, and known as oil of almond, has been used as cooking oil. Kernels contain between 2.05% and 2.40% hydrogen cyanide, but consumption after proper processing is sufficient to reduce harmful effects.{{Clarify|does this book really discuss plants grown in central Asia and around the Mediterranean?|date=January 2011}}
